<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Annazid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">990 CE – 1117 CE (approx.) · capital Hulwan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish dynasty that controlled the Zagros passes between Iraq and Iran from its base at Hulwan for over a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fath-muhammad-ibn-annaz">Abu'l-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz</a>, chief of the Kurdish Shadhanjan tribe, established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulwan">Hulwan</a> on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> around 990, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> authority weakened. The Annazids competed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hasanwayhid-dynasty">Hasanwayhid dynasty</a>, taking over much of their territory after 1015.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Annazids were tribal rulers who controlled key passes, collecting tolls and tribute and playing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kakuyid-dynasty">Kakuyids</a> and other neighbours against each other. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-shawk-faris">Abu'l-Shawk Faris</a> extended their power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinavar">Dinavar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a>, but family feuds were frequent. Their towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulwan">Hulwan</a>, Dinavar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrizor</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The arrival of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> in the 1040s brought repeated invasions, and the Annazids became Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. They lost their last strongholds around 1117.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 990 CE – 1117 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.40%;width:2.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>990 CE – 1117 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hulwan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Annaz, Abu'l-Shawk Faris</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Annazids, Banu Annaz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1054&amp;focus=annazid-dynasty" aria-label="Annazid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5846 930 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Annazid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6166 1186L6367 1352M6166 1186L6481 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6367 1352h0M6481 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6166 1186h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6166 1186h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hulwan. <a href="/atlas?year=1054&amp;focus=annazid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
